  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "Emma felt a pang of regret; she had been forced to observe the proper courtesies toward someone she could not bring herself to like for three long months—always doing more than she wished and less than she ought. Why she disliked Jane Fairfax was a hard question to answer; Mr. Knightley had once suggested it was because she saw in Jane the truly accomplished young woman she herself wanted to be regarded as. Though Emma had vehemently denied that at the time, there were moments of self‑examination when her conscience could not fully exonerate her. She could never seem to become acquainted with Jane: there was a coldness and reserve—an apparent indifference whether she pleased or not—that Emma could not explain, and her aunt’s endless chatter only added to the fuss that everyone made about her. It had always been assumed that the two, being the same age, must be intimate and fond of each other. Those were the only reasons she could offer—she had none better.\n\nThe dislike was so slight that every imagined fault was blown up by fancy; she never saw Jane after any considerable absence without feeling that she had somehow offended her. Now, when the expected visit finally occurred after a two‑year interval, Emma was especially struck by the very appearance and manners she had been undervaluing for those years. Jane Fairfax was exceedingly elegant—remarkably so—and Emma herself prized elegance above all. Her height was pleasing, just enough that most would call her tall, though not excessively so; her figure was graceful, her size a most becoming medium, between plump and thin, though a faint hint of ill‑health suggested a possible flaw. Emma could not help noticing all this, and then her face—her features—held more beauty than Emma remembered. It was not regular, but it was a most pleasing beauty. Her eyes, a deep grey, framed by dark lashes and eyebrows, had always earned praise; the skin, which Emma had previously criticized for lacking colour, now showed a clear, delicate complexion that needed no added bloom. It was a style of beauty whose chief characteristic was elegance, and as such Emma felt she must, out of principle, admire it—elegance, whether of person or mind, was something she saw so little of in Highbury, where distinction and merit, not vulgarity, reigned.\n\nIn short, during the first visit, Emma sat looking at Jane Fairfax with a double sense of satisfaction: the pleasure of seeing her and the feeling of doing justice, and she resolved that she would no longer dislike her. When she considered Jane’s history, her circumstances, and her beauty; when she thought of what this elegance was destined for, from where it might arise, and how she would live, she found herself unable to feel anything but compassion and respect. Especially, if one added the very likely prospect of an attachment to Mr. Dixon, which Emma had naturally begun to suspect, the admiration grew still stronger."
}

