r, now showed a clear, delicate complexion that didn’t need any added flush. It was a kind of beauty whose dominant quality was elegance, and as such, Emma felt she ought, by all her principles, to admire it—elegance, whether of appearance or mind, was something she saw so little of in Highbury. There, without being vulgar, was distinction and merit.\n\nIn short, during that first visit, Emma sat looking at Jane Fairfax with a mixed feeling of satisfaction—both pleasure and a sense of doing justice—and decided she would no longer dislike her. When she considered Jane’s background, her circumstances, and her beauty; when she thought about what all that elegance meant for Jane’s future, how she would manage, how she would live, it seemed impossible not to feel compassion and respect. Especially when, added to every known detail that made Jane interesting, was the very likely possibility of an attachment to Mr. Dixon, which Emma had naturally begun to suspect."
