{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_028",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "I have already enjoyed such success in two petitions for pardon that I risk becoming overly confident about yours, and about those of your acquaintances who might have any cause for grievance. You must all strive to grasp the precise circumstances of my arrival at Randalls; you must view me as a man bearing a secret that had to be guarded at any cost. That was the fact. Whether I was entitled to place myself in a position demanding such concealment is another matter, which I shall not treat here. For my inclination to deem it a right, I point every doubter toward a brick house with sash windows below and casements above in Highbury. I dared not address her openly; my difficulties in the then state of Enscombe were too well known to need definition, and I was fortunate enough to succeed before we separated at Weymouth, persuading the most upright female mind in creation to bend in charity toward a secret engagement. Had she declined, I should have gone mad. Yet you will be ready to ask, what was my hope in doing this? What did I anticipate? Anything and everything—time, chance, circumstance, gradual influences, sudden eruptions, perseverance and fatigue, health and illness. Every prospect of good lay before me, and the first blessing was secured when she promised fidelity and correspondence. If further explanation is required, I have the honor, dear madam, of being your husband’s son, and the benefit of inheriting a disposition to hope for good that no inheritance of houses or lands can ever match. Imagine me, then, under these conditions, arriving on my first visit to Randalls; and I am aware of a fault, for that visit might have been paid for sooner. You will look back and see that I did not come until Miss Fairfax was in Highbury; and as you were the one slighted, you will forgive me at once; but I must appeal to my father’s compassion, reminding him that as long as I was away from his house, I lost the blessing of knowing you. My conduct during the very happy fortnight I spent with you, I hope, did not expose me to reproach, except on one point. Now I come to the principal, the only significant part of my behaviour while attached to you, which causes me anxiety or demands careful explanation. With the greatest respect and warmest friendship, I mention Miss Woodhouse; my father perhaps will think I ought to add, with deepest humility. A few words he uttered yesterday revealed his opinion, and some censure I acknowledge I am liable to. My conduct toward Miss Woodhouse, I believe, exceeded what was proper. To preserve a concealment so vital to me, I was led to employ more intimacy than was permissible in the situation we were thrust into. I cannot deny that Miss Woodhouse was my apparent object—but I am sure you will accept my declaration that, had I not been convinced of her indifference, I would not have been driven by any selfish motives to continue."
}

