  "rewritten_text": "I have already succeeded twice in asking for pardons, and now I may be too sure about yours and about any offense your friends might have committed. Please try to understand exactly why I was in such a difficult position when I first came to Randalls. I had a secret that I had to protect at all costs. That is the fact. Whether I was justified in putting myself in a situation that required such secrecy is another matter, and I will not argue it here. To explain why I felt it was my right, I point to a brick house in Highbury with windows set low on the ground floor and higher on the upper floor. I could not speak to her openly; the problems I faced at Enscombe were already well known and needed no further explanation. Fortunately, before we separated at Weymouth, I managed to win the most upright woman's heart to agree, in charity, to a secret engagement. Had she refused, I would have gone mad.\n\nYou may ask what I hoped to achieve by all this, what I was looking forward to. The answer is everything: time, chance, circumstance, slow developments, sudden bursts, perseverance, fatigue, health, and illness. Every possible good seemed within reach, and the first blessing was gaining her promise of faith and letters. If you need more detail, remember that I am your husband's son, and I have inherited a hopeful disposition that no property or land can match. Imagine me arriving on my first visit to Randalls under these conditions. I feel guilty because that visit could have happened earlier. You will see that I did not come until Miss Fairfax was in Highbury, and since you were the one slighted, I hope you will forgive me at once. I must also appeal to my father's compassion, reminding him that while I was away from his house I lost the blessing of knowing you. During the very happy fortnight I spent with you, I hope my conduct did not give you cause for criticism, except perhaps in one area.\n\nNow I come to the most important part of my behavior while I was with you, the part that makes me anxious and that needs a careful explanation. With the deepest respect and friendship, I must mention Miss Woodhouse. My father might think I should add an apology for any humiliation I caused. Yesterday he said a few words that revealed his opinion, and I accept that I may be subject to some censure. I think my conduct toward Miss Woodhouse was more forward than it should have been. In order to protect the secret that was so vital to me, I allowed a level of intimacy that went beyond what was proper. I cannot deny that Miss Woodhouse was the apparent object of my attention, but I am sure you will believe me when I say that if I had not been convinced of her indifference, I would not have been driven by selfish motives to continue."
